  ],
  "opening": "In Chapter 7, Deutsch's algorithm used phase kickback and interference to determine a global property of a one-bit function in a single query. Many computational problems, however, ask us to find a specific input \\(x\\) among \\(N = 2^n\\) candidates that satisfies a condition \\(f(x) = 1\\).",
  "opening_continuation": "We first study this search problem on four items and see how phase marking and reflection concentrate amplitude on the solution. We then analyze the geometry of the search for any \\(N\\) and extend the method to general initial states.",
  "intro_continuation": "In Chapter 7, we saw how a phase oracle flips the sign of the amplitude on any input \\(x\\) with \\(f(x) = 1\\). By itself, however, changing the sign of an amplitude from \\(+1/\\sqrt{N}\\) to \\(-1/\\sqrt{N}\\) leaves its measurement probability unchanged at \\(1/N\\). How can subsequent operations turn that sign change into a high probability of observing the marked item?"
}
